\title {Boxes, Minipages and Lengths}
\author {The texish project}
\date {June 2026}
\maketitle

\section {Horizontal boxes}

An \texttt{mbox} keeps its content on one line, so a phrase like
\mbox{Newton--Raphson} never breaks across a line end. A \texttt{makebox} gives
that box an explicit width and aligns its content inside.

\medskip
\noindent\fbox{\makebox[0.5\linewidth][l]{flush left}}

\noindent\fbox{\makebox[0.5\linewidth]{centred}}

\noindent\fbox{\makebox[0.5\linewidth][r]{flush right}}

\section {Paragraph boxes}

A \texttt{parbox} sets a whole paragraph into a box of a chosen width, so two
columns of text can sit side by side on one line. Here are two parboxes, each a
third of the line wide, with a gap between them:

\medskip
\noindent\parbox{0.3\linewidth}{This is the left parbox. Its lines wrap inside the box width, and the box sits in the running text as a single object.}\hskip 24pt \parbox{0.3\linewidth}{This is the right parbox, the same width. Both are aligned on their vertical centres, the default for a parbox.}

\section {Minipages}

The \texttt{minipage} environment is the same idea written as a block. The two
below are top-aligned so their first lines share a baseline:

\medskip
\noindent\begin{minipage}[t]{0.44\linewidth}
A minipage is a parbox you write as an environment, which reads better when the
content is more than a few words --- a sidebar, a boxed note, one column of a
two-column row.
\end{minipage}\hskip 24pt\begin{minipage}[t]{0.44\linewidth}
This second minipage is top-aligned with the first, so the two blocks start on
the same line even though they have different amounts of text.
\end{minipage}

\section {Lengths}

A length is a dimension variable. \texttt{newlength} declares one, \texttt{setlength}
assigns it, and \texttt{addtolength} adjusts it --- and the result drives real
spacing.

\newlength{myindent}
\setlength{myindent}{20pt}
\addtolength{myindent}{20pt}

\noindent\hskip\myindent This line is indented by \texttt{myindent}, which was set
to 20pt and then grown by another 20pt, so the indent is 40pt.

\section {Conditionals}

\texttt{ifthenelse} chooses a branch on a test. \texttt{equal} compares strings,
and the comparison operators compare numbers:

\medskip
\noindent Three is \ifthenelse{\> {3} {2}}{greater}{not greater} than two.

\noindent The words match: ``yes'' is \ifthenelse{\equal{yes}{yes}}{equal}{unequal}
to ``yes''.

\vfill\eject
